            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 4 — Electricity: Magnetic and Heating Effects"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.893,
            0.03,
            0.918,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "47"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.744,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It was the day of the science exhibition, and the school was buzzing with energy. Mohini and Aakarsh, along with their friends, went from one exhibit to another, eagerly exploring different models, asking questions and taking notes. One simple model really fascinated them. It was a working model of a lifting electromagnet which was displayed by their senior, Sumana. In it, instead of a hook like a typical crane, there was an iron nail wrapped with a wire, which was connected to a battery. When Sumana closed the circuit, the nail picked up iron paper clips like a magnet. When she opened the circuit, the clips fell off. Mohini and Aakarsh, were surprised. They remembered learning earlier (in the chapter ‘Exploring Magnets’, Curiosity, Grade 6) that magnetic materials were attracted by magnet and that iron was a magnetic material. But in Sumana’s model, there was no magnet, only an electric circuit. They were so excited that they wanted to try it out themselves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.4,
            0.726,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.1 Does an Electric Current Have a Magnetic Effect?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.482,
            0.532,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 4.1: Let us investigate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.524,
            0.694,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Collect a magnetic compass, an electric cell, a cell holder, two drawing pins, a safety pin, two nails, two pieces of connecting wires (one longer and one shorter), and two small pieces of cardboard."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.604,
            0.693,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Using two drawing pins, a safety pin, and a cardboard piece, make a switch (as you made it earlier in the chapter ‘Electricity: Circuits and their Components’ in Curiosity, Grade 7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.704,
